**`src/components/discover/OpportunityQueue.tsx:214-216`** — `rankOpportunities()` (O(n log n) sort + string building) runs unmemoized on every render, including on every busy-flag toggle from 4 different mutations:
```tsx
// Before
const ranked = rankOpportunities(rows, (o) => ...);
// After
const ranked = useMemo(() => rankOpportunities(rows, (o) => ...), [rows, themeById]);
```
The file already memoizes `themeById` and two other derived values — this is the one that fell through.

**`OpportunityRow.tsx`** — not wrapped in `React.memo`; `OpportunityQueue.tsx:256-283` passes it fresh inline arrow functions for every callback on every render, so memoizing the row alone won't help without also `useCallback`-stabilizing the handlers.

**`src/hooks/use-machine-view.tsx:36-39`** and **`use-flow-mode.tsx:228-242`** — both context providers build an unmemoized value object and an inline closure each render, unlike `WorkspaceProvider`/`AskProvider` in the same codebase, which already follow the correct `useMemo`/`useCallback` pattern (good reference to copy from).

**`src/lib/studio.functions.ts:561-562`** — two independent, data-independent DB round-trips run sequentially instead of in parallel:
```ts
// Before
const builder = await costAndStatusByMission(runRows);
const other = await costAndStatusByMission(otherRunRows);
// After
const [builder, other] = await Promise.all([costAndStatusByMission(runRows), costAndStatusByMission(otherRunRows)]);
```

**`ci-poll-tick.ts:78-81`** — `resolveGitHub()` (a multi-query auth-resolution chain) re-runs per changeset even when several belong to the same workspace; memoize by `workspaceId` in a `Map` before the loop.

Frontend: the global `QueryClient` (`staleTime: 30_000`, `refetchOnWindowFocus: false`) and per-surface polling are already well-configured — no refetch-storm pattern found.

**`src/lib/ai/studio-ci-logs.server.ts:120-142`** — sequentially `await fetch`es each failing CI job's log tail in a `for` loop, compounded by being called once per changeset inside the already-sequential poll-tick loop:
```ts
// Before: sum-of-N latency
for (const c of failing) { await fetch(`.../jobs/${c.id}/logs`); }
// After: max-of-N latency
await Promise.allSettled(failing.map(c => fetch(`.../jobs/${c.id}/logs`)));
```

**`src/lib/studio.functions.ts:806-818`** (`getStudioSession`) — fetches the 20 most-recent CI tool_calls globally for the user, then linear-scans in JS for one matching `pr_number`. This is both wasteful and fragile — a busy user with multiple concurrent builds can push the right row out of the 20-row window, silently dropping the CI snapshot. Fix: filter by `.in("trace_id", traceList)` (already computed a few lines above, and an indexed column) instead of scan-then-filter. This one's a correctness bug, not just a perf issue.

---
No missing indexes found beyond what's covered above — the query shapes seen are already backed by existing indexes. Nothing else worth flagging surfaced from either audit; both agents explicitly called out well-batched reference implementations (`build.functions.ts`, `WorkspaceProvider`) worth copying the pattern from elsewhere.

